Consider relativistic heavy-ion collisions at intermediate center-of-mass energies per nucleon pair (roughly a few GeV up to a few tens of GeV), where neither a purely viscous-hydrodynamic-plus-hadronic-afterburner hybrid description (appropriate at very high energies) nor a purely hadronic transport description (appropriate at very low energies) is uniformly valid throughout the collision volume.

Develop a single dynamical framework that smoothly interpolates between these two limits. Concretely, formulate a consistent space-time evolution model in which:

(i) regions of sufficiently high local energy density are treated by relativistic viscous hydrodynamics with a prescribed equation of state, while regions of lower density are treated by a microscopic hadronic transport (Boltzmann-type) description, with a well-defined dynamical interface (for example, a hydrodynamic core surrounded by a transport "corona", or localized hydrodynamic "bubbles" embedded inside a transport calculation, or an equivalent hybrid construction);

(ii) the matching between hydrodynamic cells and transport particles conserves energy, momentum, and conserved charges (baryon number, electric charge, strangeness) locally, and reduces to the standard high-energy hybrid model and to the standard hadronic transport model in the appropriate limits;

(iii) the equation of state used in the hydrodynamic regions incorporates a QCD phase transition and a critical end point, including non-equilibrium effects associated with critical slowing down, finite-size and finite-time effects, and fluctuations of the order parameter near the critical point.

The problem is to construct such a framework explicitly, prove (or demonstrate) that it provides a consistent interpolation between the two limiting descriptions, and specify how the phase transition and critical point — together with their non-equilibrium dynamics — are to be implemented within it.

